From: LightMoss <noreply@github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/wsi/display: turn a panel mounted rotated with the primary plane

The Mesa counterpart of LightMoss's SDL2 patch of the same name
(board/rpminiv2/patches/sdl2), for programs that present through
VK_KHR_display (RetroArch's khr_display context, SDL's KMS/DRM Vulkan
windows).

The Retroid Pocket Mini V2's AMOLED panel scans out portrait (1080x1240)
and is mounted landscape; its device tree says so ("rotation = <90>"),
which the kernel reports as the connector's "panel orientation" (Right
Side Up). VK_KHR_display showed the display as the CRTC's 1080x1240 and
every Vulkan program came out sideways.

Where the CRTC's primary plane can rotate (with ROCKNIX's SDE rotator
patches the SM8250's planes offer 90 and 270), turn it by the panel's
orientation, as the kernel's fbdev emulation does for 0 and 180, and
show programs the display with its sides swapped (1240x1080): the mode
list, the display's resolution, the plane capabilities, the surface's
current extent and present rectangle. Programs render into images of
that size, which the plane scans out turned: the kernel's legacy
drmModeSetCrtc() and drmModePageFlip(), which this WSI uses, swap the
source size of a primary plane turned by 90 or 270 degrees.

- The panel's rotation is decided once per connector, when it is first
  enumerated: Left Side Up 90, Upside Down 180, Right Side Up 270, as
  SDL's patch does, and only if the primary plane of the CRTC driving
  the connector offers it.
- The plane is the CRTC's own primary plane: the one bound to it, else
  the CRTC's index among the primary planes (MSM's DPU lists every
  plane as possible on every CRTC and gives CRTC i the i-th primary),
  else the first usable one; looked up again for the CRTC the mode set
  uses.
- It is turned with drmModeObjectSetProperty() right before each
  drmModeSetCrtc() (DRM master needed; without it nothing changes and
  the WSI waits for its VT as before), and turned upright again when a
  swapchain is destroyed or the display released once the CRTC shows
  none of its images (removing them disabled the plane), so the next
  program finds it as it was; the next present then sets the CRTC
  again, turning the plane first. The kernel's fbdev restore resets it
  too.
- A plane that refuses (other than for want of DRM master) is set
  upright (an earlier mode set may have turned it) and leaves the panel
  unturned and its sides unswapped from then on. The swapchain made for
  the swapped size no longer fits the mode: its present returns
  VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR without trying the mode set, and the
  application's next swapchain, of the size the surface now reports,
  is shown unturned (sideways). A refused 180 changes no sizes, so the
  mode set goes ahead.

MESA_KMS_PANEL_ROTATION=0 leaves the panel alone; 90, 180 or 270 turn
it by that many degrees (counter-clockwise, as DRM counts). An SDL
Vulkan window needs SDL and Mesa to agree: set SDL_KMSDRM_PANEL_ROTATION
to the same value.

On displays without a "panel orientation", or one that says Normal,
nothing changes.
---
